course: dictionary
lesson: 8590
title: Food words and context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When you meet a word about food you do not know, look at the words around it. Context clues often tell you what kind of thing the word is, even if you have never tasted it. «A British biscuit is an American cookie; an American biscuit is a soft bread roll a bit like a scone.» The words «a bit like» tell you a scone is a kind of soft baked thing, like a roll.
explain: Look for clues about taste, shape and what people do with the food. A word that goes with butter, jam and cream is probably a baked treat, not a meal of meat or vegetables. Then check: does your meaning fit the sentence? If you swap in «a small, round baked cake», does the sentence still make sense? If it does, you have probably found the meaning.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. In Australia and New Zealand, people sometimes use scone as an informal word for the head, as in «Use your scone!» Look at the rest of the sentence to decide which meaning fits. Here, «use your» suggests a part of the body, so it is the head, not a cake.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A scone is a small, slightly sweet, round baked cake, often eaten with butter, jam and cream.
fact: A cream tea is a meal of scones with jam and clotted cream, often with a cup of tea.
fact: In North America, a scone is often sweeter and shaped like a triangle.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, informal speech uses scone to mean the head: «Use your scone!»
exercise: «We ate warm scones with strawberry jam and cream.» What is a scone? | A type of wild bird that sings at dawn | *A small, round baked cake eaten with butter, jam and cream | A loud noise made by a drum | A long, thin piece of wood for a fire
exercise: «An American biscuit is a soft bread roll a bit like a scone.» What does «a bit like» tell you? | Scones are never made with flour | Scones are bigger than castles | A scone is a kind of cookie | *Scones and American biscuits are similar baked foods
exercise: In Australia, Grandma says, «Use your scone!» What does she mean? | Share your scone with a friend | *Use your head | Put the scone in the oven | Eat your scone quickly
test: What goes on a scone in a cream tea? | *Jam and clotted cream | Salt and pepper | Ketchup and mustard | Ice and sugar syrup
test: Which phrase means a meal of scones, jam and cream, often with tea? | fruit scone | soft bread roll | *cream tea | cheese scone
exam: Which of these best describes a scone? | A loud musical instrument played in a band | A kind of bird that lives near the sea | *A small, slightly sweet, round baked cake | A large, heavy stone used to build walls
